WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.130
AGV: Let's say you
or your company

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.440
built an application
a while back,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.030
and now you would
like to bring it up

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:10.140
to snuff and offer features
that modern apps have today.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.180
For example, you
may want to enable

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.270
voice ordering or
mobile payments,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.610
or you may want to make it
easy for other developers

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.510
to integrate into your app.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.260
Or maybe you would like to
create a unified experience

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.140
across different lines of
business and multiple back end

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.610
services.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.270
In this episode, I will cover
how Google's API management

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.240
tool, called Apigee,
helps organizations create

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.970
digital experiences
for both older back end

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:40.590
services, microservices, hybrid
or multi-cloud environments,

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.345
and fully serverless stacks.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.800
API stands for Application
Programming Interface,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.440
and it's like a library
of functions that passes

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.150
information over the web.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:01:00.340
It defines the inputs and
outputs available over HTTP.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.430
For example, let's say you
are a logistics company,

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.319
and you would like to
enable your partners

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:07.720
to integrate your
package tracking service

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.090
into their e-commerce website.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.870
You then create an API
that takes shipment details

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:18.590
as inputs and returns a
tracking ID in the response.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.070
Another API uses the
tracking ID to check

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.570
the status of the package.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.610
This now makes your
service portable.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.330
You can create services
not just for partners,

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:31.960
but for your customers,
employees, vendors, et cetera.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.700
There are two
developer audiences

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.030
that most benefit from
an API management tool.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.770
There are those that
build and manage

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:43.360
APIs, known as the producers,
and those that use the APIs,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:43.870
a.k.a.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.100
consumers.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.320
Now, the challenge
to the producer

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.430
is to design an easy-to-use
API, keep developers abreast

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.380
of changes, and
ensure things do not

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.570
break as functionality evolves.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.020
They also have to consider
three core design components

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.690
such as how will I prevent
unauthorized access

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.310
to keep things secure?

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.230
How compatible
will my services be

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:08.060
when working with other
platforms and devices?

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.120
And finally, how can
I measure that things

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.590
are running smoothly?

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.940
As you can quickly see, that's a
lot of layers to consider here.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.800
Then there are
consumers of APIs who

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.920
need to easily discover
and find the APIs they need

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.920
and then quickly
understand how to use them.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.920
And API is really only
useful if it can easily

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.020
be adopted by other developers.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.990
So with these audiences
in mind, Apigee

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.320
helps manage the entire
lifecycle of an API.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.850
Let me discuss
three key pillars.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.430
The first is it
offers a supportive

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:43.390
portal for other developers
to discover your APIs,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.210
rapidly understand
them with the intention

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.890
that they can build
applications with them.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.790
The second is monitoring
and analytics.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.950
These are available so
that your API program

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:57.970
managers can see which
APIs are used most and how.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.850
You can also receive
alerts when appropriate.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.310
The third is the
API runtime, which

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.320
executes the policies
you configure

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.200
for security, traffic
management, and efficiency

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.700
of your APIs.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.490
What is especially nice is
that you can make changes

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.860
without disrupting
users because Apigee

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.640
is a proxy that isolates the
app developer from your back end

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:19.720
service.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.960
For example, you can change
your database implementation,

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.120
move your services
to a new host,

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.480
or make any other changes to
the service implementation,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.170
and by maintaining a
consistent front end API,

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.240
existing client
apps will continue

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.110
to work regardless of how
changes occur in the back end.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:41.170
You also create one
or more API proxies

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:45.850
that map publicly available
URLs to your back end services.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.600
A proxy is the HTTP
endpoint on Apigee

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.600
that developers use to access
your back end services.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.580
You can optionally
apply policies

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.970
on a proxy to also add
functionality to a service

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.940
without having to make any
changes to the actual back end

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.360
service such as data
transformations, filtering,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.980
conditional logic, custom
code, and many other actions.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.810
The important thing
to remember is

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.840
that you implement
policies on Apigee, not

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.120
on your back end server.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.600
Next, you bundle the API
proxies and publish them

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.089
in an API product.

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:24.580
Next, you let your
developers know that the API

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.440
product is available.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.170
And once app developers
know about the availability

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.990
of your API product,
they can register

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.110
as an app developer
for your API product,

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:39.940
create an app to obtain an
API key for that API product,

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.730
and finally make requests
to your services,

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.670
passing the API key
with each request.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:46.780
And there you have it.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:48.850
A quick walkthrough
on how Apigee

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.610
helps create robust
digital experiences

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:54.430
by leveraging APIs
and having a tool

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.740
set and portal to manage them.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.570
If you would like
to learn more, this

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:01.240
is only episode one of
a series of short videos

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.770
to help you get started.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.210
Also, in this
video's description,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.250
we have several helpful links.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.590
And friends, if you found
